On Fri, 01 Aug 2003 12:23:17 GMT, Richard Bos wrote:

> "Mirick" <infinitum@chello.se> wrote:
[...]
>> Anyone have any tips about Samurais? What to do, what to use.. I think I got
>> the hang of it, but I'm probably missing stuff.
[...]
> 
> Don't bother getting your short sword skill up. Invest in long sword
> (katana) skill and in two-weaponing. 

I second this. You don't need to advance bow either. As Boudewijn Waijers
pointed out: you should be quicker than most monsters. So you can safely
afford a few more shots to kill, in general. I like to train martial arts,
long sword and two-weaponing. Later on I invest a bit in clerical spells
and maybe riding.

> Start sacrificing as soon as
> possible until you get Snickersnee, which is a katana and your
> guaranteed first weapon. Then two-weapon Snickersnee and your original
> katana. If you can get Frosty early, even better - both Brands, being
> long swords, use the same skill as your katanas. Failing that, you can
> dip for Excalibur if you like. It's a bit better than Snickersnee, but
> not nearly as good as a Brand.

Early in game Excalibur seems much more useful for me than Brands or
Snickersnee. The searching helps to avoid traps. So you can go to the
(upper) mines early. It makes chatting with succubi/incubi safer. And 
it is rustproof from the beginning. Later you can switch to the Brand 
you come across. 

Be sure to have some kind of teleport-away handy while #dipping. The
level should be cleared of hostile monsters. And there should be some 
way to run in wide circles...  ;-)

> Once you get a silver saber, it might be worth getting that up, too, for
> Gehennom, but getting enough skill to reach Gehennom in the first place
> is more important...

Once in Gehennom you should be very dangerous to any kind of undead. 
For security two-weapon Excalibur if you like. But don't waste your
precious skill slots for saber.
 
> Don't bother with your quest artifact, either. It may be a big bad
> two-handed sword, but it's no patch on two well-enchanted long swords or
> katanas. 
[...]

Very true! The Tsurugi is too dangerous to be wielded or even carried
openly. I remember Rodney fetching it off my hands once. He only needed 
one hit.  :-(  That's why I wouldn't bother enhancing two-handed
weapon, too.

Be sure to carefully pick your armor. A samurai is a bad spell-caster. 
So you should wear dragon armor (GDSM will be fine ;-)  ), [GoD, [HoB
and a robe if you find them.

What else? - Always follow Bushido.  ;-)

